<!-- GameCore qualification probe preservation roots (docs/game-core/04-unity-integration.md section 8 item 4).

     Managed stripping High runs UnityLinker over every managed assembly. Two different mechanisms keep the probe's
     registrations alive, and this file only covers the second one:

     1. Reachability from generated roots. The generated catalogs hold direct references and survive because real
        code uses them, not because of this file:
          * `Assets/GameCore.Validation/Generated/ProbeCatalog.g.cs` registers `NarrativeFamilyEntryKey` ->
            `new GameCore.Validation.Slices.NarrativeFamilyPluginEntry()`, whose constructor calls the narrative
            package's own `NarrativeRegistration.Systems()` / `Readers()` / `Messages()`, `NarrativeDeclarations`
            and `NarrativeRegistrations`. That is what keeps `GameCore.Rules.Narrative`,
            `GameCore.Gameplay.Narrative` and `GameCore.Gameplay.Narrative.Fixtures` in a stripped player since
            GC-012, together with the probe host's own call sites into the narrative scenario.
          * `Assets/GameCore.Validation/GeneratedCards/CardCatalog.g.cs` registers `CardFamilyEntryKey` ->
            `new GameCore.Validation.Slices.CardFamilyPluginEntry()` plus the card plugin factory, the registered
            reducer, the registered predicate and the four system factories. `GameCore.Rules.Cards` and
            `GameCore.Gameplay.Cards` are therefore reachable without any entry in this file.

     2. Explicit preservation. Only entry points that no managed call site can keep alive, and the two test-only
        fixture assemblies whose whole purpose is to be linked but not referenced:
          * `GameCore.Unity.Adapters`: the PlayerLoop nodes are invoked by native code through
            `updateDelegate`/`RuntimeInitializeOnLoadMethod`, so nothing managed references them.
          * `GameCore.Validation.Fixture` and `GameCore.Unity.Fixtures`: the GC-001 negative-registration proof and
            the W1/W2 gate fixtures are deliberately reached only through generated-style factory keys resolved at
            runtime, and GC-001's acceptance requires a linked-but-inactive plugin whose factory nothing else
            calls.

     Removing the GC-001-era `preserve="all"` entries for the narrative gameplay and rules assemblies was the point
     of GC-012: reachability must come from generated roots, and a stripped type would be fixed by correcting
     generation rather than by widening this file. -->

<linker>
  <assembly fullname="GameCore.Validation.Fixture" preserve="all" />

  <assembly fullname="GameCore.Unity.Fixtures" preserve="all" />

  <!-- GC-005 owned worlds and guarded dispatch. The application pump is reached only through a PlayerLoop
       updateDelegate, which native code invokes: no managed call site keeps it alive, so the bootstrap, the
       installer, the pump and the reset entry point are preserved explicitly. -->
  <assembly fullname="GameCore.Unity.Adapters">
    <type fullname="GameCore.Unity.Adapters.GameCoreApplicationBootstrap" preserve="all" />
    <type fullname="GameCore.Unity.Adapters.GameCorePlayerLoopInstaller" preserve="all" />
    <type fullname="GameCore.Unity.Adapters.GameCoreApplicationPump" preserve="all" />
    <type fullname="GameCore.Unity.Adapters.GameCoreApplicationReset" preserve="all" />
    <type fullname="GameCore.Unity.Adapters.GameCoreApplicationComposition" preserve="all" />
    <type fullname="GameCore.Unity.Adapters.GameCorePumpLoop" preserve="all" />
  </assembly>
</linker>
